  "difficulty": 3,
  "pace": 1200,
  "brief": "`nums` is a list of whole numbers, which may repeat and may be negative. Write `first_missing_positive(nums)`, which returns the smallest positive whole number that is not in `nums`.",
  "constraints": [
    "$0 \\le n \\le 200{,}000$ entries, each between $-10^9$ and $10^9$.",
    "$O(n)$ expected time is the target. Sorting is allowed, but checking each candidate with a scan is too slow.",
    "Each test has a 5-second limit."
  ],
  "hint": "Put every entry in a hash set. Then count up from 1 until a number is missing. The answer is never above $n + 1$.",
  "starter": "#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_set>\n\nint first_missing_positive(std::vector<int> nums) {\n    // your code here\n    return 0;\n}\n",
  "examples": [
